{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"06228e37-b676-4094-82ad-431a39ce0d0d","status":"SUCCESS","response":"The HR corridor generator was fixing a key‑name mismatch:\n\n### Change made\n- **File:** [`gen.py`](file:///tmp/h-7Noulh/gen.py)  \n  - Updated `hr_target` to return `{ \"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi }` instead of `{ \"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi }`.\n\n### Why the change was needed\n- The Intervals.icu API interprets `{value, end}` as a *zone‑mapped* (Lap‑HR) target, which the FIT export then clamps to the watch’s own LTHR zones.  \n- Using `{start, end}` creates an *instant‑HR corridor* that preserves the raw BPM values calculated from the athlete’s LTHR, so the watch displays the exact corridor we planned.\n\n### Parts of the colleague’s note that applied\n- **Key name switch:** The note explained that `start` (not `value`) changes the export mode to instant‑HR.  \n- **Use `{start, end}` for instant HR:** Followed the guidance to use this shape.  \n- **Avoid `{value, end}`:** Recognized that this shape caused the clamping issue.\n\n### Result\nRunning `gen.py` now generates HR corridor objects that Intervals exports correctly, and the Garmin watch will show the intended BPM range.\n\nFor a full summary, see the generated **ANSWER.md**: [`ANSWER.md`](file:///Users/valentinakoniukhova/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/06228e37-b676-4094-82ad-431a39ce0d0d/ANSWER.md).\n","duration_seconds":31.199294,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":11303,"output_tokens":2045,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":91968,"total_tokens":13348}}}
